Wagon door controller
=====================

Setup
-----
- Deploy this script on every locomotive and car in the train.
- Orient this programmable block with its Forward axis toward the wagon's
  chosen front and its Right axis toward the wagon's right side.
- Add [DOOR_LEFT] to the Custom Name of every door on the wagon's local left
  side, and [DOOR_RIGHT] to every door on its local right side. These sides are
  defined while looking toward the programmable block's Forward direction.
- Tagged doors must be on the same grid as this programmable block. A door with
  both tags is ignored.
- Add (train coupler) to the Custom Name of each modded train connector.
- Add (train light) to the Custom Name of the four train lights on each car:
  two on each end.
- Add exactly one of (side 1) or (side 2) to every tagged light and connector.
  Use the same side tag for the lights and connectors at the same end of a car;
  side 1 and side 2 may be assigned arbitrarily on each car.
- Do not put both side tags on one block. Lights without exactly one side tag
  remain off; a locomotive needs tagged blocks on both ends to declare a
  direction.
- Place the programmable block so its Forward axis points toward the chosen
  front of its car.

Locomotive and door commands
----------------------------
Run door commands on a programmable block in the train. Run the `principale`
and `inactive` commands only on the locomotive's programmable block:
- forward / backward
- open left / right / both
- close left / right / both
- switch left / right / both
- principale sens 1 / principal sens 1
- principale sens 2 / principal sens 2
- inactive

The active locomotive's Forward axis defines sense 1; sense 2 is the opposite.
The script maps that direction to the correct side tag on the locomotive, then
relays it through the tagged couplers. A new active declaration replaces the
previous one. The active locomotive can clear its state with inactive; running
inactive on another car does not clear the current active locomotive.
Direction and door commands travel over connected (train coupler) blocks, so
no train ID configuration is needed.
Running forward or backward on the active locomotive changes its declared
direction to sense 1 or sense 2.
After coupling or uncoupling cars, run the active declaration again to
redistribute the train direction.

Headlights
----------
The side tag identifies each light's end; the active direction propagated
through the couplers determines whether that end is front or rear. A coupled
train connector turns off the lights with the same side tag. At an open end,
the front lights are white with a 50 m radius and the rear lights are red with
a 1 m radius. If no locomotive is active, both open ends show red lights. The
side mapping is relayed car to car, so it follows the consist through curves
and loops.

Tagged doors and lights must be on the same grid as this programmable block.
Tagged train couplers must also be on this grid. The door controller compares
the car's orientation with the command direction and swaps door sides when the
car is facing backward. If the orientation is ambiguous, it closes tagged
doors instead of guessing.

Tagged door blocks are enabled while moving to their commanded state, then
disabled once fully open or closed. Players cannot operate disabled doors.

This orientation limitation applies to door-side selection: it works when each
car's heading is within 60 degrees of the command's heading (or its opposite
when reversed). Headlight direction is propagated neighbor-to-neighbor through
the couplers and does not use this global heading comparison.
